# Wiring: `synthorg.{{.Domain}}` service

Generated by `synthorg new service {{.Domain}}`. The Python files
under `src/synthorg/{{.Domain}}/` and
`src/synthorg/observability/events/{{.Domain}}.py` are
conventions-clean on their own (ruff, mypy, every `scripts/check_*.py`
gate). The steps below plug the new service into application boot;
applying them is a one-time manual edit per scaffold.

## 1. Wire `EXCEPTION_HANDLERS` (only if the domain raises)

`{{.ClassName}}Error` and `{{.ClassName}}NotFoundError` already
inherit from existing handlers (`DomainError` / `NotFoundError`), so
the default mapping in `src/synthorg/api/exception_handlers.py`
already covers them. Add a domain-specific handler entry only when you
introduce a custom `error_code` and need a tailored response shape;
see `docs/reference/errors.md` for the registration recipe.

## 2. Start the service at boot

Add to `src/synthorg/api/lifecycle_helpers.py` (or the equivalent
factory the surrounding subsystem uses):

```python
from synthorg.{{.Domain}}.service import {{.ClassName}}Service

async def _start_{{.Domain}}_service(app_state: Any) -> {{.ClassName}}Service:
    service = {{.ClassName}}Service()
    app_state.{{.Domain}}_service = service
    asyncio.create_task(service.run())
    return service
```

Then call `_start_{{.Domain}}_service` from the boot routine that
hosts other long-running services. Without this step the service is
unreachable -- the `unwired-settings` /
`check_setting_to_startup_trace.py` gate will catch any dangling
settings, but a service without a setting flies under the radar; only
add a setting once the service has a real configurable knob.

## 3. Add real workload to `run()`

The generated `run()` blocks on the stop event so the service is a
no-op until you replace the body with the real workload. Remember:

- Use `self._clock` for any time read or sleep (lets `FakeClock`
  drive deterministic tests).
- Add new event constants to `synthorg.observability.events.{{.Domain}}`,
  never bare string literals in `logger.*` calls.
- New error subclasses go in `synthorg.{{.Domain}}.errors` rooted at
  `{{.ClassName}}Error`.
